# Hot path invariants

Code inside the fixed-rate loop body runs 250 times per second with a hard
deadline. A single violation of any rule below shows up as a spike in the p99.99
tail, which is the exact number this repo exists to report. These are not style
preferences.

Inside the loop, nothing may:

- allocate or free. No `new`, no `malloc`, no `push_back`, no `std::string`
  construction, no `std::vector` resize, no `std::function`. Buffers are sized
  and touched before the loop starts.
- perform I/O. No `printf`, no `std::cout`, no `fprintf`, no logging call of any
  kind. stdio takes a lock and can fault a page. Samples go into the
  preallocated `RingLog` and are written after the run ends.
- take a lock or block. No mutex, no condition variable, no `std::async`.
- throw. Exception unwinding is unbounded. Return status, do not throw.
- call anything whose worst-case duration you cannot state.

Additional standing rules for `src/`:

- Timing uses monotonic clocks only. `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` on Linux,
  `CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW` on Darwin. Never wall clock, never `std::chrono::system_clock`:
  an NTP correction mid-run corrupts the whole dataset.
- Sleeps are absolute, never relative. `clock_nanosleep(TIMER_ABSTIME)` and
  `mach_wait_until`. A relative sleep folds each wake error into the following
  period and the loop drifts roughly 3.5% slow, measured.
- Platform differences live behind `rt_platform.h` and nowhere else. No
  `#ifdef __APPLE__` in loop or estimator code.
- Privilege failures downgrade to a recorded warning, never an abort. The same
  binary must run unprivileged on a laptop and privileged on the RT host, and
  the CSV header records which configuration actually applied.
- Prefer `-O2` over `-O3` and keep the generated code legible.

Mark the loop body with sentinels so the pre-commit hook can scan it:

    // HOT PATH BEGIN
    // HOT PATH END

When reviewing this directory, check for these violations first and name them
explicitly. That is the most useful thing you can do here.
